<TopicPage slug="subjects/public-relations/foundational/what-public-relations-is">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Public Relations, and it assumes no background in marketing,
    journalism or communication theory. Everything is built from everyday experience — you have
    been the audience of public relations your whole life, which means you already know its
    outputs better than you know its name. This lesson names them, orders them, and shows the
    machinery behind them.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define public relations precisely, and say how it differs from advertising and marketing;</li>
    <li>outline the profession's history from press agentry to the two-way, research-based model;</li>
    <li>name the functions a public relations department performs, from press relations to crisis management;</li>
    <li>explain the four channel families — paid, earned, shared, owned — and how they fit together;</li>
    <li>segment an organisation's <em>publics</em> and say why no organisation ever has just one audience.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever a message appears, we ask two questions — <em>who paid for it?</em> and
    <em>who could have stopped it?</em> The answers separate the channel families more cleanly
    than any jargon.
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to be mentioned</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose a bakery wants the city to know its bread is exceptional. It can buy a
    billboard — the message runs exactly as written, on schedule, in front of everyone, and
    every reader knows the bakery paid for it, so most readers discount it accordingly. Or a
    food journalist can visit unannounced, be impressed, and write a rave review the bakery
    never saw before publication. The second route reaches fewer people, costs nothing but the
    bread, and moves opinion far more — precisely because a third party chose to say it and
    the bakery could not control a word.
  </p>
  <p>
    That contrast — control versus credibility — is the door into the whole discipline.
    Advertising buys guaranteed, controlled messages and pays a credibility price for it.
    <strong>Publicity</strong> — information about an organisation that appears in the news
    media and is not directly paid for by the organisation — earns credibility but surrenders
    control, and it can turn bad without warning. Public relations is the profession that
    manages that trade: it is the practice of building and keeping relationships between an
    organisation and all the groups that can affect its future, using honest communication
    as the main instrument.
  </p>

  <h2>What public relations is — and why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    The current definition of the Public Relations Society of America (PRSA) is worth reading
    slowly: public relations is "a strategic communication process that builds mutually
    beneficial relationships between organizations and their publics". Three words carry the
    weight. <em>Strategic</em>: communication chosen on purpose, planned and measured, not
    improvised goodwill. <em>Mutually beneficial</em>: the relationship must serve both sides —
    a purely one-sided persuading campaign is exactly the caricature the definition rejects.
    <em>Publics</em>, plural: the employees, investors, regulators, journalists, customers and
    communities with a stake in the organisation, each needing its own relationship.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does a profession exist for this? Because organisations live on
    <strong>legitimacy</strong> — the ongoing social permission to operate — and legitimacy is
    granted by groups the organisation cannot command. Journalists decide whether to cover it;
    regulators decide how tightly to supervise it; employees decide whether to believe its
    promises; a community accepts or resists its presence. Every organisation communicates
    whether or not anyone manages it; public relations is the professionalisation of that
    communication into strategy — with the ethical weight that follows, because a profession
    that shapes public discourse has obligations to the discourse itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> public relations is strategic, two-way relationship management
    with every group that matters to an organisation — and its hardest instrument is publicity
    it does not control. Next, how the profession got from its carnival beginnings to that
    definition.
  </p>

  <h2>Definitions and history: from press agentry to relationships</h2>
  <p>
    The profession's history explains both its caricature and its cure. The late nineteenth
    century was the age of <strong>press agentry</strong> — publicity at any price, accuracy
    optional, epitomised by showmen who would print anything that drew a crowd. The turn came
    in the early 1900s, when corporate scandals and muckraking journalism demanded something
    better. <strong>Ivy Ledbetter Lee</strong>, often called the father of modern PR, published
    his <em>Declaration of Principles</em> (1906): the public deserves accurate information,
    the press deserves open access, and the work should be done in the open. His advice during
    the Pennsylvania Railroad's 1906 derailment — invite journalists to the scene, issue honest
    statements — demonstrated that candour could be strategy, and his later counsel to the
    Rockefeller family after the 1914 Ludlow Massacre showed that listening, even belatedly,
    beat silence.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="scrub the milestones from press agentry to dialogue" widget="timeline" config={prHistoryTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>Edward Bernays</strong>, Freud's nephew, professionalised the persuasion side: he
    wrote the field's first textbooks (<em>Crystallizing Public Opinion</em>, 1923), taught its
    first university course, and coined the title "counsel on public relations" — while also
    staging campaigns (the 1929 "torches of freedom" walk, which reframed cigarettes as
    women's liberation) that embody both the power and the ethical peril of engineered consent.
    The modern synthesis came with James Grunig and Todd Hunt's <em>Managing Public
    Relations</em> (1984) and the long "excellence" research that followed. Their four models —
    press agentry, public information, two-way asymmetric, two-way <strong>symmetrical</strong>
    — read the profession's history as a progression toward dialogue: excellent practice
    researches its publics, negotiates with them, and lets the organisation be changed by what
    it hears, because relationships built on one-way persuasion do not survive contact with a
    sceptical public. As a description of daily practice the ideal is imperfect, but as a
    <em>definition</em> it holds the field's centre — and it is why measurement has migrated
    from clip counts to trust.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that PR means spin — deceptive reframing of facts. It seems
  reasonable because the profession's showman ancestors really did work that way, and because
  "PR" is used casually to mean whitewash. But spin that unravels is a <em>crisis</em>, and
  crisis response is the discipline's most scrutinised product; the profession's hard-won
  lesson is that honesty is a strategy rather than a virtue tax. The ethical spine is codified
  in the PRSA Code (advocacy, honesty, expertise, independence, loyalty, fairness) precisely
  because the field's failure mode — misleading publics — destroys the only asset it manages.</p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="find the faulty line — a 'what PR is' briefing" widget="spot-error" config={prSpinSpot} />

  <h2>What the function actually does</h2>
  <p>
    In practice, "public relations" names a family of jobs. A typical department or agency
    performs some or all of these: <strong>press relations</strong> — placing newsworthy,
    accurate information with journalists so an organisation is covered fairly;
    <strong>product publicity</strong> — publicising specific products or services;
    <strong>corporate communications</strong> — creating the internal and external messages
    that present the organisation coherently; <strong>public affairs</strong> — building and
    maintaining community and government relationships; <strong>lobbying</strong> —
    communicating with legislators and officials to promote or defeat legislation;
    <strong>employee and investor relations</strong> — maintaining trust with the workforce and
    the financial community; and <strong>crisis management</strong> — responding when events or
    unfavourable publicity threaten the organisation's standing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each function by the public it faces" widget="sort-buckets" config={prJobsSort} />
  <p>
    The workhorse tool is the <strong>press release</strong> — a formal announcement of a
    newsworthy event (a new programme, a product launch, an appointment) written in news style
    and offered to journalists. Offered is the operative word: a release is a proposition of
    newsworthiness, not a guaranteed story, and newsrooms rewrite, query or discard most of
    what they receive. Around the release sit the supporting trades — furnishing speakers for
    civic groups, writing speeches for executives, planning events, coordinating the
    organisation's response when publicity turns hostile. Publicity itself is neither good nor
    bad by default; it is simply coverage you did not pay for — and coverage is the one thing
    in this discipline that can turn against you overnight.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="fill the blank — what a release actually is" widget="cloze" config={prReleaseCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a press release is a news story — that issuing one
  means the announcement "is in the media". A release is an <em>offer</em> of newsworthiness
  written in news style; journalists treat it as a starting point, rewrite or discard most of
  what lands in their inboxes, and publish only what they judge genuinely newsworthy. That
  gatekeeping is not friction in the system — it is the source of the credibility earned
  coverage carries.</p>

  <h2>PR versus advertising and marketing</h2>
  <p>
    The cleanest distinction is <strong>control versus credibility</strong>, and the numbers
    make it vivid. Advertising is paid communication that identifies a brand, placed exactly as
    approved. Its scale is astonishing and its cost per contact can be tiny: a 30-second
    commercial during the 2021 Super Bowl cost roughly 6 million dollars, but with nearly 96
    million viewers the price per exposure was about 6 cents. What money cannot buy in
    advertising is the endorsement itself — audiences know the message was paid for, and they
    discount it accordingly. Earned coverage runs the other way: a journalist's story, an
    expert's citation, a customer's review carry weight precisely <em>because</em> the
    organisation did not buy them — and carry risk advertising never touches, since the story
    may run against you. Marketing, meanwhile, is the larger commercial discipline of moving
    products to customers (product, price, place, promotion); public relations is broader than
    its promotional slice because it serves relationships with <em>all</em> publics —
    including those who will never buy anything, such as regulators and neighbours.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the price per exposure" widget="estimate" config={prSuperbowlEst} />
  <p>
    The modern working map for all of this is the <strong>PESO model</strong>: <strong>Paid</strong>
    (advertising and sponsored content — precise, scalable, credibility-discounted),
    <strong>Earned</strong> (coverage you did not pay for — credible because a third party
    chose it), <strong>Shared</strong> (social conversation, reviews, shares — participation
    you can join but not command), <strong>Owned</strong> (your website, blog, newsletter —
    full control, lower trust, and the backbone the other channels point back to). The widget
    below walks the four.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four channel families (PESO)" widget="equationStep" config={pesoCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the two questions from the opening — <em>who paid?</em>
    and <em>who could have stopped it?</em> — sort every panel. Paid: the organisation paid,
    and controls everything. Earned: nobody paid, and the journalist could have stopped it.
    Shared: nobody paid, and the audience could stop or amplify it. Owned: the organisation
    controls the platform but cannot compel anyone to believe it. The strategic skill is
    orchestration — moving one true story across families so each does what it does best.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that any publicity is good publicity. The saying survives
  because early press agents lived by it, but publicity is simply unpaid coverage — and unpaid
  coverage of a scandal is the least controllable asset in the field. What the saying gropes
  toward is that <em>responding well</em> to bad publicity can build more durable trust than
  the bad coverage destroyed — which is a claim about crisis management, not about coverage
  being welcome.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — the publicity saying" widget="judge" config={prPublicityJudge} />
  <p>
    Measurement is where the distinction became enforceable. The industry's legacy metric —
    advertising value equivalency (valuing a news story as if the space had been bought) — was
    formally discredited by the <strong>Barcelona Principles</strong> (first declared 2010,
    reaffirmed since): measure <em>outcomes</em> (behaviour, trust, relationship quality), not
    <em>outputs</em> (impressions, clip counts). A product launch buys reach (advertising's
    job), courts reviews and analyst coverage (earned), hosts the canonical facts on its own
    site (owned) and monitors community reaction (shared) — and the PR team's report is not
    "we reached 4 million people" but "trusted third-party coverage reached the audiences
    advertising cannot convince, and sentiment held through launch week".
  </p>

  <h2>Stakeholders and publics: the plural that defines the field</h2>
  <p>
    An organisation never has one audience, and PR's first analytical act — before any writing
    happens — is identifying and prioritising its <strong>publics</strong>. Employees,
    investors, regulators, journalists, customers, suppliers, neighbours and activist groups
    each hold different stakes, use different channels and bring different scepticism; a
    message that reassures investors (efficiency gains) can enrage employees (their jobs) in
    the same sentence. Grunig's <strong>situational theory of publics</strong> supplies the
    segmentation logic: a public forms around a problem when three conditions align — people
    <em>recognise</em> the problem, <em>feel involved</em> in it, and believe they are
    <em>not constrained</em> from acting on it. The same layoff announcement therefore
    produces a latent public (employees unaware their division is exposed), an aware public
    (staff who have read the rumour mill) and an active public (the works council and the
    journalists covering the restructuring) — three audiences needing three strategies, not
    one press release. The wider circle — everyone with a stake, aware or not — is the
    organisation's <strong>stakeholders</strong>; mapping the movement from stakeholder to
    active public is situational analysis.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each public stage with its case" widget="match" config={prPublicsMatch} />
  <p>
    Publics discipline everything downstream, and a plant-closure case shows the craft: the
    regulator gets compliance-first notification and complete documentation; employees hear
    before the market does (internal publics are the classic first audience betrayed, and
    leaks are the standard punishment); the affected community gets consequences, mitigation
    and timeline; investors get the strategic rationale — four audiences, four framings of the
    <em>same true facts</em>, sequenced so that no public learns something destabilising from
    another's channel. The two-way discipline applies throughout: each public's channels are
    monitored, feedback is channelled into the decision, and the PR function acts as the
    organisation's <em>boundary spanner</em> — the role that carries the outside world's
    judgements inward, not just the organisation's messages outward.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A newspaper writes a critical story about a company's safety record, using company data. Is that publicity? Who controlled it?</li>
    <li>State the two questions that separate the PESO channel families, and use them to classify a company blog post.</li>
    <li>Why did the Barcelona Principles reject advertising value equivalency?</li>
    <li>An employer announces redundancies. Why can one announcement not serve all its publics?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Yes — publicity is unpaid media coverage, favourable or not.
    The journalist controlled it, which is exactly why it is credible. (2) Who paid for it, and
    who could have stopped it? A blog post is owned: the company paid and controls it — lower
    trust, but the one channel that cannot be taken away or misquoted. (3) Because it valued
    earned coverage as if the space were bought — an output proxy that rewards column
    millimetres rather than outcomes such as trust, behaviour and relationship quality. (4)
    Because each public holds a different stake: employees hear about their livelihoods,
    investors about strategy, the community about local consequences, regulators about
    compliance. One framing will be destabilising or irrelevant for at least one of them —
    though every framing must rest on the same true facts.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Public relations is strategic communication that builds mutually beneficial relationships between an organisation and its publics.</li>
    <li>The control–credibility trade-off separates PR from advertising: bought messages are controlled and discounted; earned coverage is credible and uncontrollable.</li>
    <li>The profession evolved from press agentry (publicity at any price) through Ivy Lee's transparency and Bernays' professionalisation to Grunig's two-way symmetrical ideal.</li>
    <li>The function spans press relations, product publicity, corporate communications, public affairs, lobbying, employee and investor relations, and crisis management — with the press release as its workhorse tool.</li>
    <li>The PESO model (paid, earned, shared, owned) maps the channels; orchestration across them is the strategic skill.</li>
    <li>Publics are plural and situational: recognise, feel involved, not constrained — a public becomes active when all three hold.</li>
    <li>Measure outcomes (trust, behaviour, relationships), not outputs (clippings, impressions).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson fixed the map; the next lessons teach the machinery. Research, planning and
    measurement (unit 2) turn publics and objectives into programmes — formative research,
    strategy, tactics, evaluation. Writing and channels (unit 3) supplies the craft: news
    releases, media kits, digital and internal communication. Media relations then goes inside
    the newsroom you will be pitching. Every later skill answers to the distinctions fixed
    here: know your publics before your messages, and count the relationship, not the
    clippings.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Crisis communication is where all of this converges under maximum pressure. Situational
    crisis communication theory (SCCT, developed by Timothy Coombs) matches response strategy
    to responsibility: crises the organisation clearly caused (a fatal product defect known
    about internally) demand full apology, compensation and corrective action; crises with
    ambiguous attribution permit justification and context; crises the organisation is a victim
    of (rumour, tampering by others) can be met with denial of responsibility — but matching
    wrong is catastrophic, most famously when a denial or a legalistic shrug meets evidence of
    organisational fault. The working rules are the profession's distilled scar tissue: respond
    fast (the first hours frame every later story), say what you know and what you do not
    (speculation exposed as false destroys the credibility you will need tomorrow), centralise
    the message while decentralising the listening, and treat employees as a primary public —
    the workforce learning of its own company's crisis from the news is a wound the
    organisation pays for permanently. Crisis practice, read closely, is the symmetrical model
    under duress: research what publics are actually saying, tell the truth, respect that each
    public holds its own stake, and count trust retained or destroyed.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="walk the response — SCCT under pressure" widget="branch" config={prCrisisBranch} />
  <p>
    Three currents define the field's present. <strong>The critical
    counter-tradition</strong>: scholars of persuasion note that real PR is predominantly
    <em>asymmetric</em> — advocacy for clients within contests (regulatory battles, union
    disputes, litigation) — and that the symmetrical ideal functions partly as
    self-description; understanding both registers is part of the education, because the
    ethical line (disclosure, no fake grassroots or "astroturfing", no undisclosed paid
    advocacy) is where practice and principle must actually meet. <strong>Trust as the master
    variable</strong>: decades of institutional-trust surveying (the Edelman Trust Barometer is
    the standard reference) show trust migrating away from institutions toward "people like
    me" and employees-as-witnesses — which has quietly restructured the craft toward credible
    action over polished statements: trust the deed more than the release. <strong>The
    platform transformation</strong>: when shared media can make any stakeholder a broadcaster,
    the news cycle compresses to minutes and the journalist-gatekeeper model weakens — making
    the oldest PR virtues (speed, accuracy, consistency across publics) survival equipment.
    The definition, though, has not moved: strategic communication, building mutually
    beneficial relationships with publics, measured in trust.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> PRSA Code of Ethics and Certificate in Principles of Public Relations content; Dietrich, <em>PESO model</em>; Grunig &amp; Hunt, <em>Managing Public Relations</em>. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

